 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "System",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "ZH is a wire-to-board connector on 1.5 mm pitch: crimp contacts on AWG #32 to #26 wire go into a ZHR receptacle housing, which plugs into a shrouded header on the board and can be unplugged. Mated, a top-entry pair stands 5.6 mm tall and is 3.5 mm thick. The headers also accept the ZR insulation-displacement connectors."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Construction",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The housing has contact retention lances. Each contact has a dimple at its centre that bears on the header post. Listed features: compact and low profile, housing lances, reliable contact construction, distortion-resistant construction."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Header styles",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Headers come with 2 to 13 circuits. Through-hole: top entry (B..B-ZR) and side entry (S..B-ZR), each also with 3.4 mm tails (-3.4). SMT: SM2 side entry (S..B-ZR-SM2-TF), held by two locating bosses; SM4 top entry (B..B-ZR-SM4-TF) and side entry (S..B-ZR-SM4A-TF), held by a solder tab at each end. S6B-ZR-SM4A-TF is the six-circuit SM4 side-entry header: contact row A = 7.5 mm, body B = 12.0 mm."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Through-hole board pattern",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Seen from the solder side, holes are φ0.7 ±0.03 on 1.5 ±0.05 pitch. The ±0.05 pitch tolerance is non-cumulative and holds for every centre. Hole size varies with board type and piercing method; boards of hard material take larger holes. The dimensions are guidelines; JST gives details."
  },
  {
   "topic": "SMT board pattern",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Patterns are seen from the component side. Pad pitch is 1.5 ±0.05, non-cumulative, and signal pads are at least 0.7 wide. SM4 headers have one solder-tab pad at each end of the row. SM2 headers have a φ1.1 +0.1/0 hole per end for the locating bosses, each 0.9 ±0.05 beyond the end contact along the row, so the holes sit 1.5×(N-1)+0.9 from one end contact to the far hole, N being the circuit count. The figures are guidelines; JST gives details."
  }
 ],
